# Book and author
body=ph('The book','Bulletproof <em class="accent">Knowledge.</em>','Building Hallucination-Resistant Neurosymbolic Agents with Lean and Logic Programming.')+f'''<div class="container page-content"><div class="book-page"><img src="/assets/bulletproof-knowledge.jpg" alt="Bulletproof Knowledge cover" width="305" height="457"><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">By Volodymyr Pavlyshyn</p><h2 style="margin-top:0">What may your agent remember?<br>What may it claim?</h2><p>An agent’s memory can preserve a mistake long after the conversation that created it. This book explores an engineering response: make accepted state changes and answers carry evidence for explicit rules.</p><p>Begin with Lean types and a small ontology. Introduce dependent types, invariants, higher-order logic, and λProlog. Build toward an agent whose symbolic checks can reject a proposed fact or answer and explain which obligation failed.</p><div class="actions">{link(BOOK,'Get the book on Leanpub ↗','btn')}{link(AMAZON,'Amazon US ↗','btn outline')}</div><p><small>The Amazon link uses the supplied book identifier on amazon.com. Availability and formats are shown by the retailer.</small></p><h2>Three ideas to take into your work.</h2><ol><li><strong>Model knowledge precisely.</strong> Give entities, relations, and admissible states an explicit type-level contract.</li><li><strong>Separate search from checking.</strong> Allow a flexible component to propose evidence while a smaller trusted component checks it.</li><li><strong>State the limit honestly.</strong> A derivation is relative to the chosen definitions and axioms. It does not turn an unverified observation into a true one.</li></ol><div class="callout">Companion code, examples, and build instructions are available in the public repository. Follow its pinned toolchain when running the book’s examples.</div><p>{link('https://github.com/Volland/bulletproof-knowledge','Explore the companion repository ↗')}</p><p>{link(BOOK,'Read the description, contents, and free sample on Leanpub ↗')}</p></div></div><section class="section"><h2>Keep a graph book beside it.</h2><div class="resource-grid"><article class="resource-card"><div class="meta">Typed knowledge graphs</div><h3>TypeDB for Edge AI Agents</h3><p>Domain relationships, typed knowledge, and graph memory for agents.</p>{link('https://leanpub.com/typedbforedgeaiagents','Read on Leanpub ↗')}</article><article class="resource-card"><div class="meta">Embedded property graphs</div><h3>LadybugDB for Edge Agent AI Memory</h3><p>Property graphs, Cypher, metagraphs, and local agent memory.</p>{link('https://leanpub.com/ladybugdb','Read on Leanpub ↗')}</article></div></section></div>'''
page('book','Bulletproof Knowledge','The book by Volodymyr Pavlyshyn on hallucination-resistant neurosymbolic agents with Lean and logic programming.',body,'/book/')
body=ph('About the author','Volodymyr <em class="accent">Pavlyshyn.</em>','Software architecture, knowledge graphs, and AI systems with explicit foundations.')+'''<div class="container page-content"><div class="author-layout"><img src="/assets/author.jpg" alt="Portrait of Volodymyr Pavlyshyn" width="500" height="600"><div class="prose"><h2 style="margin-top:0">From connected information<br>to dependable knowledge.</h2><p>I’m a software architect and author working on agent systems, knowledge graphs, memory architectures, and trust infrastructure. My work connects practical software engineering with the models that make a system’s behavior understandable.</p><p>Lean 4 Knowledge brings together a particular strand of that work: using typed graph APIs, formal semantics, and logic programming to make the boundary between a proposal and a justified conclusion explicit.</p><p>The projects here explore different parts of the problem. TypeDB and LadybugDB provide graph foundations. LeanRDF gives semantic claims a formal meaning. LeanElpi supplies higher-order logic programming and search. Lean provides a language in which to state and check the obligations.</p><p><em>Bulletproof Knowledge</em> develops these ideas into a practical path for building agents whose memory and answers are constrained by evidence.</p><div class="actions"><a class="btn" href="https://pavlyshyn.me/">Personal website ↗</a><a class="btn outline" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/pavlyshyn/">LinkedIn ↗</a></div><h2>Explore the work.</h2><ul><li><a href="https://github.com/Volland">Open-source projects on GitHub</a></li><li><a href="/book/">Bulletproof Knowledge and related books</a></li><li><a href="https://pavlyshyn.me/">Architecture, writing, and professional background</a></li><li><a href="mailto:pavlyshyn@gmail.com">Contact by email</a></li></ul><p><small>Biographical context and portrait from <a href="https://pavlyshyn.me/">pavlyshyn.me</a>. Legal provider details appear in the <a href="/impressum/">Impressum</a>.</small></p></div></div></div>'''
page('author','About Volodymyr Pavlyshyn','Meet the author of Bulletproof Knowledge and the Lean graph engineering projects.',body,'/author/')
